The largest startup penalty is the lifecycle itself: start destroys the process container before recreating it. Docker Compose can reconcile an existing
  project without that step. The preferred model is:

  - start: create or update only what changed, then start/resume services.
  - stop: stop containers but retain them.
  - A future explicit cleanup command may remove containers and caches.

  ## Highest-priority improvements

  1. Stop destroying containers during start

  core/scripts/start.js:43 unconditionally calls stop. The current core/scripts/stop.js:10:

  - Lists every Docker container.
  - Inspects them sequentially.
  - Force-removes matches.
  - Runs a global docker system prune.

  This is slow, discards reusable container state, affects unrelated Docker projects, and makes the following compose up rebuild work Docker could have reused.

  Recommended:

  - Remove the implicit stop call from start.
  - Let docker compose up -d --remove-orphans reconcile changes.
  - Make stop use docker compose stop, retaining the container.
  - Never prune as part of ordinary start or stop.

  This should produce the largest immediate speed improvement.

  6. Reduce bind-mount count

  API startup recursively finds every directory and symlink and mounts them individually in core/scripts/startApi.js:133. Site startup similarly discovers and
  mounts many individual paths.

  Large numbers of overlapping mounts increase Compose generation time, container creation time, and filesystem overhead. Prefer the smallest stable parent-
  directory mounts that preserve source composition and do not hide container-owned files.

  7. Avoid recurring ownership scans

  Every start runs recursive ownership checks through core/scripts/changePermissions.js, while API setup may delete root-owned dependency directories through
  core/scripts/removeRootOwnedDirectories.js:8.

  A better solution is to ensure containers consistently run with the developer’s UID/GID and create files with correct ownership initially. Permission repair
  should be exceptional, not part of every start.

  Your approach is reasonable for your intentionally uniform development environment. Linux permissions care about numeric IDs, so aligning host dev and
  container dev at 1000:1000 is the important part; renaming node mainly improves clarity and path consistency.

  The real problem is allowing Docker to invent missing bind-mount sources. Recursive ownership repair is a valid temporary safety net, but it is expensive and
  hides incorrect mount definitions.

  My preferred direction is:

  - Create every bind-mount source explicitly as dev before Compose runs.
  - Know explicitly whether each source must be a file or directory; filename extensions are not reliable.
  - Use Compose long bind syntax with create_host_path: false, so an overlooked path produces an informative error instead of a root-owned directory.
  - Keep changePermissions temporarily until this prevention is proven reliable.

  Then the permission scan can eventually be removed safely rather than merely optimized.


  ## Error handling improvements

  Many important commands currently use runOnTerminal() without throwOnError, so failures may be silently ignored. Examples include certificate generation,
  Nginx reload, database Compose startup, search startup, and cache startup.

  Recommended behavior:

  - Critical setup commands must fail immediately.
  - Errors should include the operation, command, relevant paths, process, tenant, and captured stderr.
  - Successful operations should emit only concise phase-level confirmations.
  - Commands should not redirect useful error details to /dev/null, as certificate and Nginx setup currently do.
  - The final success output should show the process, local URL, reused/recreated status, and startup duration.

  ## Correctness issues to fix before optimization

  These are separate from performance but can make startup unreliable:

  - core/scripts/createDirectories.js:96 appends dependency mappings after the loop that processes mappings, so those entries are never created or mounted
    there.

  - core/scripts/mapNode.js:20 calls writeFileIfNotExists without importing it.
  - core/scripts/os.js:201 uses pascalize without importing it.
  - core/scripts/startAccounts.js:34 executes Stop ... as a shell command.
  - core/scripts/startApi.js:205 checks the database container name when looking for an exited search container.
  - Headless panel setup creates no Compose file.
  - hideError in core/scripts/os.js:31 does not match terminal’s hideErrors option.
  - Local DNS newline handling changes an in-memory string but does not write that newline.
  - Deterministic ports are stable but are not checked for collisions.

  ## Production-parity improvements

  - Use a shared service definition with a development override, rather than independently evolving local and production definitions.
  - Pin MongoDB, PostgreSQL, Redis, Meilisearch, and runtime image versions instead of using latest.
  - Use the same runtime entrypoints and configuration structure locally and in production.
  - Keep development-only differences limited to source mounts, watch mode, .local domains, local certificates, and exposed host ports.

  The best first implementation is redesigning start/stop to retain containers and eliminating automatic pruning. It offers the greatest speed improvement with
  a relatively small, well-contained change.


---

• The startup takes roughly 3.1 seconds, excluding nested double-counted entries.

  Main bottlenecks:

  1. Container lifecycle: about 2.1 seconds
      - Database/search setup: 1,180 ms
      - Process Compose startup: 919 ms
      - Destructive stop: 464 ms

     The stop removes the process container and globally prunes Docker. Database and search containers then require Compose startup again. Retaining containers
     remains the best first optimization.

  2. Nginx reloads: 395 ms
      - Nginx reloads three times: application, database, and search tenant processing.
      - These should be consolidated into one conditional reload.

  3. Permission repair: 187 ms
      - Most of this is the /var/tmp/api ownership scan: 139 ms.

  4. Dependency calculation: 89 ms
      - Almost entirely the shell pipeline used to discover dependencies.

  Not significant:

  - CI/CD generation: 0.985 ms
  - 1,088 bind-source checks combined: 4.965 ms
  - Localization mapping: 7.765 ms
  - Runnable mapping: 9.464 ms
  - Compose-volume generation: 13.699 ms
  - Certificate and Nginx file generation: generally below 2 ms each

  Conclusion: changing CI/CD scheduling would add complexity for a saving of only one millisecond. The first change should be removing destructive stop/prune
  behavior and retaining containers.
